## Curated at U62: run-zig 74 of 74; two re-freezes, both COMPILER-85

    run-zig              cobblestone 28 of 28 · roc 46 of 46
    freeze-upstream-ir   cobblestone 28 unchanged · roc 44 unchanged, 2 frozen
    ir-diff              cobblestone 28 same · roc 44 same, 2 differ

The two that moved are roc-tuple-two-lists and roc-tuple-same-list-twice:
`MkTup2`'s instantiated type is now `(list int-default)` where U61 closed it to
`int-default`, though its arguments are lists. That was issue 153's second
shape (posted as comment 5778393190 at U61); upstream fixed it as COMPILER-85
(main 26027). Reviewed token by token, nothing else moved; re-frozen as curated
`0f1bb41`. Note curated's default branch is `main`, not `master`.

ir-diff still says both differ, at the same token, which is `MkTup2`'s type
annotation where it is applied to `x` and `y`:

    rust        (name "MkTup2" (fn (tvar 2) (fn (tvar 3) (ctd "Tup2" (args (tvar 2) (tvar 3))))))
    U62 upstr.  (name "MkTup2" (fn (list int-default) (fn (list int-default) (ctd "Tup2" ...))))
    U61 upstr.  (fn int-default ...), the wrong instantiation COMPILER-85 fixed

Rust annotates the constructor reference with its GENERIC type (the
declaration's own variables); upstream with the type INSTANTIATED at the use.
Rust did not change and did not regress. It differed at U61 too, from a wrong
instantiation; it now differs from a right one. (An earlier draft of this
entry, and curated `0f1bb41`'s message, said the Rust frontend "has
COMPILER-85 to catch up on". That was wrong: COMPILER-85 was upstream
instantiating badly, and Rust never instantiates here.)

Whether Rust SHOULD instantiate constructor references is open: it matters
only if the Rust IR is meant to match upstream's token for token there. At U61
ir-zig (Rust IR -> zigemit -> zig, run) matched on these units, so the
uninstantiated variables break nothing downstream. Settle it with
rust-codex-compiler's own contract when that arm runs. `.rust.ir` is not
touched.

Nothing else moved, so none of the 74 units uses generic equality over a
parameterised type or a class method: the equality-helper and
method-template changes to the IR front door are invisible to this corpus.

