## Verdict: Update 62 is verified. No defect found in U62 itself.

Every arm is at U62 and green on what it grades: bare metal 5/5, both
transpiler fixed points (zig; wasm on both roads), the curated corpora on
every arm (cobblestone 28, roc 46, safari 54), safari's specs and whole app.
What U62 took of ours it took faithfully: PRs 155 and 156 verbatim, PR 154's
tests byte-identical (its backlog row dropped as the fix made it moot), and
issue 153's two shapes and issue 157's three items all acted on, the second
shape of 153 visibly in the two roc re-freezes.

**Every red this cycle was ours, and almost all of it was drift.** Our
harnesses carried hand-kept copies of upstream's build: chapter lists, the
driver's IR tail, the quire table's location, the foreword chapters the
driver cites. U62 moved each of those and each copy broke, or had already
drifted silently: the equality helpers missing from the IR since U56, and
`wasm-exports` read in a shape upstream stopped accepting at U59. The bulk of
the work was replacing those copies with upstream's own sources:

    build/compiler-order.txt        chapter lists   (cobblestone-qemu,
                                                     codex-zig-transpiler,
                                                     codex-wasm-transpiler)
    plugs/zig/build.ps1             the ring plug's compiler chapters
    compile-frontend-ir +
    prepare-method-ir               every harness's IR (three repos)
    assemble_unit's cite resolver   the driver's foreword chapters
    build/host/windows/
    quire-map.ps1                   the quire table (Rust, safari)

Two upstream changes needed a decision rather than a sync, both our own asks
landing: the ReleaseFast/ReleaseSmall refusal (issue 157) made safari's
browser module ReleaseSafe (2.6 MB stripped, from 0.7 MB), and the guard
entered cobblestone-qemu's and the transpiler's precondition lists.

**What U62 leaves for us is Rust catch-up, not upstream defects:** the four
builtins (done), four programs the port refuses and six it accepts where U62
decides otherwise, and seven runtime differences in the sweep. Listed in the
rust-codex-compiler section above.

**Out to Damian from this cycle: nothing.**

Also new this cycle: processed entirely in the cloud (see the top of this
log), and the Rust verdict is now meaning-based (sweep + check + linear).

